description: check create permission on the container an object lands in
 Patch 0002 checks the parent when creating a registry subkey, and checks the
 wrong one. A caller passes a whole path -- "Policies\Foo\Bar" from an HKLM
 handle -- so the parent registry.c sees is the handle's, not the container the
 new key actually lands in. Every component below the first goes unchecked.
 .
 lookup_named_object() in the object manager already resolves a path to the
 deepest object that exists, leaving the remainder to create, so
 create_named_object() is the one place that knows the real container. The check
 belongs there, and the registry-level one is removed as both redundant and
 wrong.
 .
 The requirement is GENERIC_WRITE on the container, mapped by the object's own
 type: "may modify this container" is the right question whether it is a
 registry key or a directory. Containers with no security descriptor still admit
 everyone -- check_object_access() says so outright -- which is what makes it
 safe to apply to every named object rather than to the registry alone.
 .
 Verified within a single server session: an administrator creates a key, and an
 ordinary user is then refused when writing to it. It does not by itself make
 the S2 gate's clause 3 pass, for a reason outside this patch -- Wine's registry
 file format stores no security descriptors at all (see save_subkeys()), so
 every descriptor is lost when the server restarts and reloads system.reg.
 Persisting them needs a separate decision; this patch is a prerequisite either
 way.
origin: Stained Glass OS
bug: https://github.com/Stained-Glass-OS/stained-glass/issues/2
forwarded: not-needed
 Stained Glass OS does not submit patches upstream; see ADR 0006.
last-update: 2026-09-22

--- a/server/object.c
+++ b/server/object.c
@@ -463,6 +463,37 @@
             return NULL;
         }
 
+        /* Stained Glass OS: creating a child must be permitted by the
+         * container it actually lands in.
+         *
+         * This is the only place that knows which that is. A caller passes a
+         * whole path -- "Policies\\Foo\\Bar" from an HKLM handle -- and
+         * lookup_named_object() above resolves it to the deepest object that
+         * exists, with new_name holding the part still to be created. Checking
+         * the *handle's* parent instead, as the obvious fix in registry.c does,
+         * guards only the first component and lets every deeper one through.
+         *
+         * GENERIC_WRITE on the container is the requirement, mapped by the
+         * object's own type: "may modify this container" is the right question
+         * regardless of whether it is a registry key or a directory.
+         *
+         * Containers without a security descriptor still admit everyone --
+         * check_object_access() says so explicitly -- so this only bites where
+         * a descriptor exists, which is what makes it safe to apply to every
+         * named object rather than to the registry alone.
+         *
+         * `current` is NULL while the server builds the registry at startup,
+         * and there is no token to check against then. */
+        if (sg_system_prefix && current)
+        {
+            unsigned int access = obj->ops->map_access( obj, GENERIC_WRITE );
+            if (!check_object_access( NULL, obj, &access ))
+            {
+                release_object( obj );
+                return NULL;
+            }
+        }
+
         new_obj = create_object( obj, ops, &new_name, attributes, sd );
         release_object( obj );
         if (!new_obj) return NULL;
--- a/server/registry.c
+++ b/server/registry.c
@@ -861,29 +861,6 @@
 
     if (!(access & KEY_WOW64_64KEY)) attributes |= OBJ_KEY_WOW64;
 
-    /* Stained Glass OS: creating a subkey must be permitted by the *parent*.
-     *
-     * Upstream never checks this, and does not need to: with one all-powerful
-     * user, the answer is always yes. It is also why a restrictive descriptor
-     * on a key does not protect it -- the check that would consult it is not
-     * reached, and the handle for a freshly created key is then allocated with
-     * alloc_handle_no_access_check(). Without this, "non-admin cannot write
-     * HKLM\Software\Policies" cannot hold however the descriptor is written.
-     *
-     * A refusal here is not necessarily an error: the caller may be opening a
-     * key that already exists, which is governed by that key's own descriptor
-     * rather than the parent's. So fall through to an open rather than
-     * failing, and let the child's descriptor decide. */
-    if (parent && sg_system_prefix)
-    {
-        unsigned int parent_access = KEY_CREATE_SUB_KEY;
-        if (!check_object_access( NULL, &parent->obj, &parent_access ))
-        {
-            clear_error();
-            return open_key( parent, name, access, attributes );
-        }
-    }
-
     if (!(key = create_key_object( &parent->obj, name, attributes, options, current_time, sd )))
         return NULL;
 
